'''Cyndi Ann Munson''' (born May 24, 1985) is an American politician who has served as a member of the [[Oklahoma House of Representatives]] from the 85th district since 2015. A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], she has served as minority leader of the Oklahoma House of Representatives since 2022.

In 2015, Munson won a special election to replace [[David Dank]], securing over 54% of the vote and becoming the first [[Asian American]] woman to serve in the [[Oklahoma State Legislature]]. Prior to her election, the district, which includes parts of [[Oklahoma City]], had been held by Republicans. She is the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] nominee for [[governor of Oklahoma]] in the [[2026 Oklahoma gubernatorial election|2026 election]].

== Early life and education ==
Munson was born in [[Monterey, California]], and raised in [[Lawton, Oklahoma]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Asian Avenue magazine - November 2015|url=https://issuu.com/asianavemag/docs/aam_nov2015/27|access-date=2020-06-21|website=[[Issuu]]|language=en}}</ref> Munson's father was a [[Vietnam War]] veteran who raised her as a single parent; her mother was born in [[Seoul]], [[South Korea]].<ref name="nondoc2025">{{cite news |title=Rep. Cyndi Munson becomes Oklahoma's first 2026 Democratic candidate for governor |url=https://nondoc.com/2025/04/15/rep-cyndi-munson-becomes-oklahomas-first-2026-democratic-candidate-for-governor/ |access-date=April 15, 2025 |work=NonDoc |date=April 15, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Hetrick |first=Haley |date=August 28, 2026 |title=Q&A: Democratic gubernatorial nominee Cyndi Munson says Oklahomans are "ready for change" |url=https://www.news9.com/oklahoma-city-news/q-a-democratic-gubernatorial-nominee-cyndi-munson-says-oklahomans-are-ready-for-change |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260901191636/https://www.news9.com/oklahoma-city-news/q-a-democratic-gubernatorial-nominee-cyndi-munson-says-oklahomans-are-ready-for-change |archive-date=September 1, 2026 |access-date=September 8, 2026 |website=[[News 9 Now and News on 6 Now|News 9]] |language=en-US}}</ref> Her parents divorced when she was 13 years old.<ref>{{Cite web |last= |date=May 18, 2021 |title=Asian American Identity, Representation, & Leadership: A Conversation with Delegate Tran & Representative Munson |url=https://www.dlcc.org/news/blog/asian-american-idenitity-representation-leadership/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260518214416/https://www.dlcc.org/news/blog/asian-american-idenitity-representation-leadership/ |archive-date=May 18, 2026 |access-date=September 8, 2026 |website=[[Democratic Legislative Campaign Committee]] |language=en |quote=My dad ultimately raised my sister and I because my mother — who is from South Korea —  they divorced when I was 13.}}</ref> Munson became the first in her family to earn a college degree, having attended the [[University of Central Oklahoma]] through [[Oklahoma's Promise]], the state's income-based tuition assistance program, where she earned a Bachelor of Arts degree in political science.<ref name="okvoice2">{{cite news |title=Munson relying on ground game, background to clinch Democratic gubernatorial nomination |url=https://oklahomavoice.com/2026/06/05/munson-relying-on-ground-game-background-to-clinch-democratic-gubernatorial-nomination/ |access-date=June 5, 2026 |work=Oklahoma Voice |date=June 5, 2026 |archive-date=June 6, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260606042926/https://oklahomavoice.com/2026/06/05/munson-relying-on-ground-game-background-to-clinch-democratic-gubernatorial-nomination/ |url-status=live }}</ref> She later earned a Master of Science in leadership education from the [[University of Nebraska–Lincoln]]. As an undergraduate, Munson participated in a program in non-profit and voluntary services at [[Georgetown University]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Representative Cyndi Munson - Oklahoma House of Representatives|url=https://www.okhouse.gov/Members/District.aspx?District=85|access-date=2020-06-21|website=www.okhouse.gov|archive-date=June 21, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200621115421/https://www.okhouse.gov/Members/District.aspx?District=85|url-status=live}}</ref>

== Career ==
Prior to entering politics, Munson worked in the nonprofit sector, including in a community engagement and development role at the OK Messages Project and with the Girl Scouts of Western Oklahoma.<ref>{{cite news |title=Rep. Cyndi Munson becomes Oklahoma's first 2026 Democratic candidate for governor |url=https://nondoc.com/2025/04/15/rep-cyndi-munson-becomes-oklahomas-first-2026-democratic-candidate-for-governor/ |access-date=April 15, 2025 |work=NonDoc |date=April 15, 2025 |archive-date=April 15, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250415164036/https://nondoc.com/2025/04/15/rep-cyndi-munson-becomes-oklahomas-first-2026-democratic-candidate-for-governor/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Munson first ran for the [[Oklahoma House of Representatives]] in 2014, but lost to the incumbent.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Felder |first1=Ben |title=Oklahoma Democrats, a once powerful party, look to rebuild |url=https://www.oklahoman.com/story/news/politics/government/2023/07/06/oklahoma-legislature-democrats-rebuilding-democratic-party/70250037007/ |access-date=17 July 2023 |work=[[The Oklahoman]] |date=July 6, 2023 |archive-date=July 17, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230717171827/https://www.oklahoman.com/story/news/politics/government/2023/07/06/oklahoma-legislature-democrats-rebuilding-democratic-party/70250037007/ |url-status=live }}</ref>
Munson was elected to the Oklahoma House in 2015, the first Asian American woman to serve in the [[Oklahoma Legislature]]. In 2019, Munson was selected to serve on the Oversight Committee for the Legislative Office for Fiscal Transparency.<ref name="AP">{{Cite web|agency=Associated Press|date=2015-09-16|title=New Oklahoma State Representative Sworn into Office|url=https://ktul.com/news/local/new-oklahoma-state-representative-sworn-into-office|access-date=2020-06-21|website=KTUL}}</ref> In 2022, Munson succeeded [[Emily Virgin]] as minority leader of the Oklahoma House of Representatives.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Felder |first1=Ben |title=Cyndi Munson, the new Democratic House leader, sees a path forward for her party |url=https://www.oklahoman.com/story/news/politics/government/2022/12/24/cyndi-munson-talks-about-the-future-of-democrats-in-oklahoma/69732340007/ |access-date=24 December 2022 |work=[[The Oklahoman]] |date=24 December 2022}}</ref> Her legislative record included criminal justice reform efforts, among them a bill to expand financial compensation available to those exonerated after wrongful convictions.<ref>{{cite news |title=Primary election will narrow crowded field in Oklahoma governor's race |url=https://www.kosu.org/2026-oklahoma-governor-primary-election |access-date=June 16, 2026 |work=[[KOSU]] |date=June 16, 2026}}</ref>

=== 2026 gubernatorial campaign ===
In April 2025, Munson announced her candidacy for [[Governor of Oklahoma]] in the [[2026 Oklahoma gubernatorial election]], becoming the first Democrat to enter the race.<ref>{{cite news |title=Rep. Cyndi Munson becomes Oklahoma's first 2026 Democratic candidate for governor |url=https://nondoc.com/2025/04/15/rep-cyndi-munson-becomes-oklahomas-first-2026-democratic-candidate-for-governor/ |access-date=April 15, 2025 |work=NonDoc |date=April 15, 2025 |archive-date=April 15, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250415164036/https://nondoc.com/2025/04/15/rep-cyndi-munson-becomes-oklahomas-first-2026-democratic-candidate-for-governor/ |url-status=live }}</ref> If elected, she would be the first woman of color to serve as [[Governor of Oklahoma]],<ref name="okvoice">{{cite news |title=Munson relying on ground game, background to clinch Democratic gubernatorial nomination |url=https://oklahomavoice.com/2026/06/05/munson-relying-on-ground-game-background-to-clinch-democratic-gubernatorial-nomination/ |access-date=June 5, 2026 |work=Oklahoma Voice |date=June 5, 2026 |archive-date=June 6, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260606042926/https://oklahomavoice.com/2026/06/05/munson-relying-on-ground-game-background-to-clinch-democratic-gubernatorial-nomination/ |url-status=live }}</ref> and the first Democrat to win the Oklahoma governorship since [[Brad Henry]] was reelected in 2006.<ref name="nondoc2025"/>

Munson's campaign centered on increasing public school funding and teacher pay, cutting taxes for lower- and middle-income residents, expanding access to healthcare, and repealing Oklahoma's near-total abortion ban.<ref name="nondoc2025"/> On the abortion issue, she pledged to put a repeal of the legislative ban to voters via ballot referendum, arguing that the ban had contributed to maternity care shortages across the state by discouraging OBGYNs from practicing in Oklahoma.<ref name="kosu">{{cite news |title=Who is Oklahoma gubernatorial candidate Cyndi Munson? |url=https://www.kosu.org/cyndi-munson-oklahoma-2026-gubernatorial-election |access-date=June 12, 2026 |work=[[KOSU]] |date=June 12, 2026}}</ref> She also pledged to reinstate diversity, equity, and inclusion programs that had been halted by the Republican-controlled legislature, and to continue backing Oklahoma's Medicaid expansion, which voters approved by ballot measure.<ref name="okvoice"/> She also called for the repeal of the state's [[parental choice tax credit]], which allows parents to claim a tax credit of $5,000 to $7,500 for enrolling children in private schools.<ref>{{cite news |title=Primary election will narrow crowded field in Oklahoma governor's race |url=https://www.kosu.org/2026-oklahoma-governor-primary-election |access-date=June 16, 2026 |work=[[KOSU]] |date=June 16, 2026}}</ref>

Munson raised over $1.1 million in total campaign contributions ahead of the primary, according to state ethics filings.<ref>{{cite news |title=Oklahoma ethics report shows over $59 million in 2026 primary campaign spending |url=https://www.news9.com/oklahoma-politics/oklahoma-primary-election/oklahoma-ethics-report-primary-campaign-spending |access-date=June 16, 2026 |work=News9 |date=June 16, 2026}}</ref> She did not make personal loans to her campaign, instead drawing on individual donor contributions and direct voter contact. The four leading Republican candidates collectively loaned more than $22 million of their own money to their campaigns.<ref name="okvoice"/><ref>{{cite news |title=Oklahoma governor candidates funnel $22 million in personal money to campaigns |url=https://oklahomavoice.com/2026/06/10/oklahoma-governor-candidates-funnel-22-million-in-personal-money-to-campaigns/ |access-date=June 10, 2026 |work=Oklahoma Voice |date=June 10, 2026}}</ref>

On June 16, 2026, Munson won the Democratic primary with approximately 74.9% of the vote, advancing to the general election against the winner of the Republican primary, [[Mike Mazzei]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Cyndi Munson wins Oklahoma Democratic Gubernatorial Primary Election |url=https://ktul.com/news/local/cyndi-munson-wins-oklahoma-democratic-gubernatorial-primary-election |access-date=June 16, 2026 |work=[[KTUL]] |agency=Associated Press |date=June 16, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Oklahoma Governor Primary Election 2026 Live Results |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-primary-elections/oklahoma-governor-results |access-date=June 16, 2026 |work=[[NBC News]] |date=June 16, 2026}}</ref>

